# M16 on the Deck, off-screen: the owner's round 4 (PLAN.md M16), with the owner's own games.
#   input-check.sh PAD m16-check.cases OUT     (input-check.sh --plan m16-check.cases shows every timeline)
# SYSTEM EMULATOR CORE|- FIRST TOKEN... -- ROM-GLOB; see input-check.sh. The run's choices land in
# OUT/home/semu.json and later cases start from them, so keep the order. Numbers are at 1280x800; the docked
# 1920x1080 cases are m16-docked.cases (SEMU_CHECK_SIZE=1920x1080 input-check.sh PAD m16-docked.cases OUT).
#
# Items 5 and 7, Fit's four states (the placement track). key:ctrl+shift+f is the radial's Fit
# (visual.placement.next) and key:ctrl+b its Bezel On/Off. Fit steps the four states in the owner's order,
# integer game (81/0), integer bezel (81/1), non-integer game (81/2), non-integer bezel (81/3), from the one a
# system starts on: a TV, the DS and the 3DS start on integer bezel (the retired fit drew the same step), a
# handheld on integer game. With the bezel off Fit steps only integer game and non-integer game. The toast
# reads FIT: <STATE> two frames before the picture moves, then FIT: <STATE> (SAME) when nothing moved. A case
# judges by its shots (before.png, after-N-*.png), the journal's 81 records and evidence.log's
# surface0_content (x,y,width,height from the bottom left).
#
# F1. Items 5 and 7, the Wii (Dolphin, the 4:3 TV). before: integer bezel, the picture 640x480 (1x of a
#     480-line frame) at 320,160, the whole TV with its stand on the table, the wall above. after-1 (81/2,
#     FIT: NON-INTEGER GAME): the picture 1067x800 at 107,0, filling the height, the TV round it cut by the
#     screen; its pixels even (sharp-bilinear: no doubled rows or columns on a scrolling scene). after-2 (81/3,
#     FIT: NON-INTEGER BEZEL): the whole TV as large as the screen holds, the picture 713x535 at 283,133
#     (283,132 from the top; the render host's lane), the stand meeting the bottom edge. after-3 (81/0, FIT: INTEGER GAME): 640x480 at 320,160.
#     after-4 (81/1, FIT: INTEGER BEZEL (SAME)): nothing moves (1x either way on 800 rows). after-5
#     (79/2, BEZEL: OFF): the picture alone on black, 640x480 at 320,160, the same size as with the TV
#     (item 7: the bezel off never shrinks the picture). after-6 (81/2, FIT: NON-INTEGER GAME): 1067x800 at
#     107,0 on black, the same size as non-integer game with the TV. after-7 (81/0, FIT: INTEGER GAME):
#     640x480 again, the two game states only. after-8 (79/0, BEZEL: LIVING ROOM CRT): the TV back round the
#     1x picture. choices wii {"bezel_variant":"tv","placement":"game"}.
wii dolphin - 60 key:ctrl+shift+f key:ctrl+shift+f key:ctrl+shift+f key:ctrl+shift+f key:ctrl+b key:ctrl+shift+f key:ctrl+shift+f key:ctrl+b -- New Super Mario Bros. Wii*.wbfs
# F2. Item 5, a 240-line TV (Beetle PSX). before: integer bezel, 640x480 (2x) at 320,160, the whole TV and
#     the table. after-1 (81/2, FIT: NON-INTEGER GAME): 1067x800 at 107,0, the CRT shader's scanlines even
#     down the picture (no bands). after-2 (81/3, FIT: NON-INTEGER BEZEL): the whole TV filling the height,
#     the picture 713x535 at 283,133 (283,132 from the top). after-3 (81/0, FIT: INTEGER GAME): 960x720 (3x) at 160,40, the TV
#     cut by the screen. after-4 (81/1, FIT: INTEGER BEZEL): 640x480 again. choices psx {"placement":"bezel"}.
psx retroarch mednafen_psx 45 key:ctrl+shift+f key:ctrl+shift+f key:ctrl+shift+f key:ctrl+shift+f -- Crash Bandicoot (USA).m3u
# F3. Item 5, a device shell (mGBA, the GBA shell). before: integer game, 1200x800 (5x) at 40,0, the shell
#     cut by the screen. after-1 (81/1, FIT: INTEGER BEZEL): the whole shell centred on black, the picture
#     480x320 (2x) at 400,267. after-2 (81/2, FIT: NON-INTEGER GAME): the picture grows from 480x320 in the
#     whole shell back to 1200x800 at 40,0, the shell cut by the screen (the press left integer bezel, so it is
#     not SAME). after-3 (81/3, FIT: NON-INTEGER BEZEL): the whole shell as large as the screen holds, the
#     picture about 546x364 at 367,249, the LCD's pixels even. after-4 (81/0, FIT: INTEGER GAME): 5x again.
#     after-5 (79/2, BEZEL: OFF): 1200x800 on black. after-6 (81/2, FIT: NON-INTEGER GAME (SAME)): with no
#     bezel Fit flips integer game and non-integer game, and 5x already fills the height, so nothing moves and
#     the toast says SAME (compared with the state the press left, integer game). after-7 (79/0, BEZEL: GAME BOY ADVANCE): the shell
#     back round the same 1200x800. choices gba {"placement":"game_fractional"}.
gba retroarch mgba 30 key:ctrl+shift+f key:ctrl+shift+f key:ctrl+shift+f key:ctrl+shift+f key:ctrl+b key:ctrl+shift+f key:ctrl+b -- Advance Wars (USA) (Rev 1).zip
# F4. Item 5, the DS shell (RetroArch melonDS): Fit never drops the shell. before: integer bezel, both
#     screens 512x384 (2x) side by side in the whole Duimon shell. after-1 (81/2, FIT: NON-INTEGER GAME): both
#     screens about 605x454, the pair spanning the screen's width, the shell cut at the sides. Each tap:
#     touch.log "-> surface 1 native" about 128,96 (the middle of the touch screen as drawn, wherever Fit
#     put it). after-3 (81/3, FIT: NON-INTEGER BEZEL): the whole shell (up to 1% past the sides), both
#     screens about 568x426. after-5 (81/0, FIT: INTEGER GAME): 2x again, the whole shell.
nds retroarch melonds 40 key:ctrl+shift+f tap:0.5,0.5 key:ctrl+shift+f tap:0.5,0.5 key:ctrl+shift+f -- Advance Wars - Dual Strike (USA, Australia).zip
# F5. Item 5 through the Vulkan layer (standalone Azahar, the 3DS shell), with taps. Numbers from the render host
#     at 1280x800 (x,y from the bottom left; top screen, then touch screen). before: integer bezel, 800x480 at
#     50,122 and 320x240 at 933,151, the whole shell. after-1 (81/2, FIT: NON-INTEGER GAME): about 858x515 at
#     0,142 and 324x243 at 956,181, the pair spanning the width, the shell cut at the sides. after-2 (tap): the
#     touch screen's middle as drawn: run.log "semu-vulkan: touch X,Y -> 1 160,120" (within a pixel or two).
#     after-3 (81/3, FIT: NON-INTEGER BEZEL): the whole shell as large as the screen holds, about 809x486 at 43,118
#     and 306x229 at 946,156. after-4 (tap): again "-> 1 160,120". after-5 (81/0, FIT: INTEGER GAME): 800x480 and
#     320x240 as in before. after-6 (81/1, FIT: INTEGER BEZEL (SAME)): nothing moves. No screen is cut in any
#     shot, no Qt arrow shows, and the shell is never dropped. choices n3ds {"placement":"bezel"}. (Supersedes
#     m15-check.cases case 12's Fit lines, written for M15's two-state Fit.)
n3ds azahar - 50 key:ctrl+shift+f tap:0.5,0.5 key:ctrl+shift+f tap:0.5,0.5 key:ctrl+shift+f key:ctrl+shift+f -- Legend of Zelda, The - Ocarina of Time 3D*.3ds
#
# Item 2, the render scale (the render-scale track). key:ctrl+shift+n is the settings radial's Render Scale
# (visual.scale.next), key:ctrl+shift+d its Restart Game and key:ctrl+m the Semu menu. Scale steps the system's
# declared render scales (journal 88/<index>/<reserved>: reserved 1 while the choice waits for Restart Game, 0
# once the emulator draws at it) and saves visual.systems.<id>.render_scale; Restart Game (87/1, RESTARTING
# GAME) relaunches once (restarts=1), and the menu opened after it reads the SCALE row's new value. The
# placement counts native pixels, so the TV and the picture keep their size and place at every scale; a higher
# scale shows as smoother edges and steadier textures under the CRT shader (which still reads native lines,
# averaged from the larger render) and as more detail with the shader off. A case judges by its shots, the
# journal's 88 records, evidence.log's game receipts (render_scale=N frames_scaled=0|1, surface0_native and
# surface0_scaled, the picture kept above native, 0x0 at 1x), the emulator's own config in OUT/home and the
# rate lines (composed frames per second: full speed is the system's own rate, 60 or 59.94, read as every line at
# least 59.8 per second with the longest gap under 25 ms, so a single dropped frame fails, as in D1 and D3).
#
# S1. The N64 through GLideN64 (RetroArch), applied by Restart Game. before: native, receipts render_scale=1
#     surface0_scaled=0x0. after-1 (88/1/1, SCALE 2X: RESTART TO APPLY): nothing restarted, the picture as
#     before. after-2 (87/1): the game boots again (restarts=1). after-3 (1): the menu's SCALE row reads 2X.
#     after-4 (5): the picture the same size and place as in before, the logo's edges smoother; receipts
#     render_scale=2 frames_scaled=1 surface0_native=313x237 surface0_scaled=626x474;
#     retroarch-core-options.cfg mupen64plus-43screensize "640x480" and mupen64plus-EnableNativeResFactor "2";
#     rate lines at 60 per second. choices n64 {"render_scale":"2x"}.
# OSD check: no RetroArch paused indicator under Semu’s menu or banner during restart.
n64 retroarch mupen64plus_next 40 key:ctrl+shift+n key:ctrl+shift+d key:ctrl+m key:ctrl+m -- Super Mario 64 (USA).zip
# S2. The GameCube on Dolphin, live. before: native, receipts surface0_native=640x480 surface0_scaled=0x0 (the
#     case's one receipt is frame 60's, before the first press). after-1 (88/1/0, SCALE: 2X): the game keeps
#     running (restarts=0) and its picture turns sharper in place; the launch log reads "semu: render scale 2x: 1
#     press(es) of Alt+Prior via X"; no Dolphin text over the picture (its Internal Resolution: 2x stays hidden);
#     the renderer's frame lines after the press read "native 640x480 scaled 1066x800 source 1066x800" (Dolphin's
#     window kept whole: its 4:3 picture is 1066 wide on 800 rows). Rate: The Wind Waker draws its title at 30
#     frames a second and Dolphin presents only the frames the game draws (D14), so every line at least 29.8 per
#     second with the longest gap under 50 ms (one and a half of its frames), before, at 2x and after: an even 30
#     is full speed (two refreshes a frame), a slower game reads under 29.8. after-2 (88/0/0, SCALE: NATIVE): back
#     to native live with one press of Alt+Next, the frame lines "scaled 0x0" again. choices gc {"render_scale":"1x"}.
gc dolphin - 50 key:ctrl+shift+n key:ctrl+shift+n -- Legend of Zelda, The - The Wind Waker (USA).rvz
# S3. The PS2 on PCSX2, applied by Restart Game. after-1 (88/1/1, SCALE 2X: RESTART TO APPLY). after-2 (87/1):
#     the game boots again. after-3 (1) and after-5 (1): the menu's SCALE row reads 2X. after-4 and after-6:
#     once the game draws, the TV and the picture the size and place they had in before (within 1 px: PCSX2's
#     own rectangle at 2x is rounded without a whole step); receipts render_scale=2 frames_scaled=1,
#     surface0_native=512x448 (Devil May Cry's GS texels halved back), surface0_source=128,16,1024,768 and
#     surface0_scaled=878x768: PCSX2's IntegerScaling (GSRenderer.cpp:358-390 at v2.6.3) shrinks the 4:3 window
#     rectangle (1067x800) to a whole multiple of the frame's texel width when that width fits it, so Devil May
#     Cry's 1024 texels draw 1024x768 at 128,16 and the renderer keeps 878x768 of it (768 rows at 512x448's
#     shape); a frame wider than 1067 texels (Def Jam's 1278) is fitted to the window, 1068x800. PCSX2.ini
#     upscale_multiplier = 2; no PCSX2 text over the picture; rate lines at 59.94 per second. choices ps2
#     {"render_scale":"2x"}.
# OSD check: Restart Game keeps the framebuffer at 1280x800 through teardown.
ps2 pcsx2 - 60 key:ctrl+shift+n key:ctrl+shift+d key:ctrl+m key:ctrl+m key:ctrl+m key:ctrl+m -- Devil May Cry (USA).iso
# S4. The PSP on PPSSPP: 2x is now the default (ppsspp.ini InternalResolution = 2; it was 0, the window's own
#     size, about 3x). before: the PSP shell and its picture as in M15. after-1 (88/2/1, SCALE 3X: RESTART TO
#     APPLY). after-2 (87/1): the game boots again. after-3 (1): the SCALE row reads 3X. after-4: the picture
#     the same size and place as in before; InternalResolution = 3; rate lines at 60 per second. choices psp
#     {"render_scale":"3x"}.
psp ppsspp - 45 key:ctrl+shift+n key:ctrl+shift+d key:ctrl+m key:ctrl+m -- Daxter*.iso
# S5. The DS through RetroArch's melonDS core at 2x, the one scale that turns on another renderer (its OpenGL one,
#     never drawn anywhere before). after-1 (88/1/1, SCALE 2X: RESTART TO APPLY). after-2 (87/1): the game boots
#     again (restarts=1); retroarch-core-options.cfg melonds_opengl_renderer "enabled" and melonds_opengl_resolution
#     "2x native (512x384)". after-3 (tap): both screens drawn in place, the same size as in before (F4 left
#     integer game: 512x384 each in the whole shell), the touch screen's text smoother; touch.log "-> surface 1
#     native" about 128,96. choices nds {"render_scale":"2x"}.
# OSD check: no load-content banner or remap-loaded notice; black outside the shell stays black.
nds retroarch melonds 40 key:ctrl+shift+n key:ctrl+shift+d tap:0.5,0.5 -- Legend of Zelda, The - Phantom Hourglass*.zip
# S6. The 3DS on Azahar at 2x. after-1 (88/1/1, SCALE 2X: RESTART TO APPLY). after-2 (87/1): the game boots again;
#     qt-config.ini resolution_factor=2. after-3 (tap): both screens the size and place F5 left (800x480 and
#     320x240, integer bezel), smoother edges (Azahar's integer window shows the 2x render at 1x on 800 rows);
#     run.log "semu-vulkan: touch X,Y -> 1 160,120". choices n3ds {"render_scale":"2x"}.
n3ds azahar - 50 key:ctrl+shift+n key:ctrl+shift+d tap:0.5,0.5 -- Legend of Zelda, The - Ocarina of Time 3D*.3ds
# S7. Reset to Default brings the render scale back, live on Dolphin (S2 left the GameCube native). after-1
#     (88/1/0, SCALE: 2X; run.log "render scale 2x: 1 press(es) of Alt+Prior via X"). after-2: the first Reset
#     press journals 85/0/1 (RESET: PRESS AGAIN), the second, a second later, 79/0 80/0 81/1 88/0/0 85/1 (RESET TO
#     DEFAULTS), with run.log "render scale 1x: 1 press(es) of Alt+Next via X", the game still running
#     (restarts=0) and the picture native again. choices gc {} (Reset removes the system's choices).
gc dolphin - 50 key:ctrl+shift+n key:ctrl+shift+r,ctrl+shift+r -- Legend of Zelda, The - The Wind Waker (USA).rvz
#
# Items 1 and 6 (the controls track). move: and tap: are the right trackpad (relative motion to a fraction of the
# touch screen as drawn, then a click for tap:); key:ctrl+shift+c is the radial's Controller Layout; bare names are
# Steam's virtual pad (the replica). Each move or tap leaves cursor-N (1.5 s after it, gamescope type 3, its cursor
# plane included) and idle-N (5 s after it), and the result line crosshair-N: ok when cursor-crosshair.sh finds
# Semu's whole crosshair centred where inject.sh put the pointer in cursor-N and not in idle-N (unchecked without
# ImageMagick on the Deck: run it on the fetched shots). Journal 83 is a layout (slot 0 Wii Remote, 1 Nunchuk, 2
# Classic, 3 GameCube; the reserved field the player minus one, plus 16 only while a layout waits for Restart Game).
#
# C1. Item 1, the crosshair on the DS (RetroArch melonDS). cursor-1, -2 and -3: Semu's crosshair, four thin white
#    arms with a black outline round a clear centre, 30x30 at 1280x800, its centre on the pointer, readable on
#    the light and the dark parts of the screen; crosshair-1, -2 and -3 ok; nothing else at the pointer (no arrow:
#    RetroArch's X cursor is blank). idle-N: no crosshair 5 s later. The centre tap still lands: touch.log
#    "-> surface 1 native" about 128,96.
nds retroarch melonds 40 move:0.5,0.5 tap:0.5,0.5 move:0.1,0.9 -- Legend of Zelda, The - Phantom Hourglass*.zip
# C2. Item 1, the crosshair on the 3DS (standalone Azahar through the Vulkan layer), as C1: crosshair-1, -2
#    and -3 ok, and cursor-N holds exactly one pointer, Semu's crosshair, with no Qt arrow beside it (Azahar's own
#    cursor is blank on its render widget while Semu draws). The centre tap: "semu-vulkan: touch X,Y -> 1
#    160,120".
n3ds azahar - 50 move:0.5,0.5 tap:0.5,0.5 move:0.9,0.1 -- Legend of Zelda, The - Ocarina of Time 3D*.3ds
# C3. Item 6, every Wii controller layout live (Dolphin), on Mario Kart Wii, which takes the Wii Remote and the
#    GameCube controller alike. By 90 s the game is in its title loop: the title (Press the A Button), its intro
#    movie, then an attract race with Press the A Button over it, round again; nothing in that loop goes past the
#    title by itself, so a shot of any other screen (a save-data prompt, the license or main menu) is an A press
#    answered. The two Controller Layouts step P1 from the default Nunchuk to Classic (journal 83/2/0, toast P1:
#    CLASSIC, run.log "P1 layout classic: 1 press(es) of Alt+Insert via X") and on to GameCube (83/3/0, toast P1:
#    GAMECUBE, never RESTART TO APPLY; run.log "P1 layout gamecube: 1 press(es) of Alt+Insert, then Alt+F11 to
#    unlink it via X"). The two souths are then the GameCube pad's A, the only input left (the Wii Remote is unbound
#    and away): the first brings an intro or attract race back to the title (or leaves the title), the second
#    leaves the title, so after-4 shows a screen past the title. The third Controller Layout steps to Wii Remote
#    (83/0/0, P1: WII REMOTE, "... then Alt+F11 to link it"). About then the attract loop may load its next demo course
#    from the SD card: the final run's "6 s at 31-53 per second, a 431 ms gap" after the link was that load, not the
#    link (M16's Deck probe, 2026-10-06: the same slowdown with no input at all at the title's white fade, Dolphin's
#    emulated CPU waiting on about 4 MB read at 1.4 MB/s while the course was not in the page cache; Alt+F11 alone
#    during a demo race slowed nothing), so two fillers wait that out; the last two souths are
#    the Wii Remote's A (and the pad's): after-8 and after-9 each show the game answering A, a screen after the
#    one before it (the next prompt or menu), never the title loop again. One Dolphin process throughout (no
#    "semu: restarting" in run.log), and no Dolphin message over the picture in any shot ("Wii Remote 1
#    disconnected", "Loading input profile"), only Semu's toasts. choices: input.systems.wii.players.1.layout
#    "wiimote" in OUT/home/semu.json. (In the VM, at a fraction of full speed, the GameCube pad's X+Y+Start held
#    with the remote away logged Dolphin's own PAD - COMBO_ORIGIN three times, and the game relinked the remote.)
wii dolphin - 90 key:ctrl+shift+c key:ctrl+shift+c south south key:ctrl+shift+c key:shift key:shift south south -- Mario Kart Wii*.wbfs
# C4. Item 1 on the Wii: one pointer, the game's own (M16's ruling: Semu draws no crosshair there, since the game's
#    IR pointer sits about 48 px above the finger and moves about 1.34 times as far). C3 left P1 on Wii Remote. At
#    60 s Mario Kart Wii plays its title loop (its intro movie draws no pointer), so the Wii Remote's A goes first:
#    the first south brings the loop back to the title (or leaves it), the second leaves the title for a screen that
#    takes the pointer (a save-data prompt, the license or main menu: after-2 is not the title loop). Then the moves:
#    cursor-3 and cursor-4 (gamescope type 3, its cursor plane included): Mario Kart Wii's own hand pointer where the
#    IR aims, no Semu crosshair and no X arrow (Dolphin's CursorVisibility 0); so crosshair-3 and crosshair-4 read
#    FAIL here (cursor-crosshair.sh finds no crosshair), which is this case's pass. idle-N: the hand pointer as the
#    game keeps it (or hidden, if the game hides an idle pointer: say which).
wii dolphin - 60 south south move:0.5,0.5 move:0.2,0.8 -- Mario Kart Wii*.wbfs
# C5. Item 1 on standalone melonDS (ES-DE's melonDS (Standalone) for the DS on Linux), which Semu does not compose:
#    no receipt, so inject.sh moves to fractions of the whole screen (its note reads "surface screen at 0,0
#    1280x800"). Semu's melonDS profile lays the screens side by side (ScreenLayout = 2, IntegerScaling: 2x, the top
#    screen at x 128-639 and the touch screen at x 640-1151, both y 208-591 on the Deck), so 0.70,0.50 (896,400) is
#    the middle of the touch screen. cursor-1 and cursor-2: melonDS's own arrow at 896,400 (it shows Qt's arrow over
#    its picture and hides it only with Mouse.Hide, which Semu leaves off; gamescope hides an idle cursor after 3 s,
#    and the shots are 1.5 s after the pointer moves), no Semu crosshair (M16's ruling, reversible: the emulator's
#    arrow stays where Semu draws no pointer, a touch screen needs a visible aim), so crosshair-1 and -2 read FAIL,
#    this case's pass; no arrow at all means melonDS or gamescope hides it: say which shot. The tap lands on the
#    touch screen in melonDS's own picture (no Semu touch.log line): the game answers it (the opening moves on to the
#    title, or the title to the file menu). S5 left the DS at 2x, so melonDS.toml has [3D] Renderer = 1 (its OpenGL
#    renderer, the only one that reads ScaleFactor) and [3D.GL] ScaleFactor = 2, run.log "Created a OpenGL context":
#    the 3D at twice the DS's lines, its edges stepping one screen pixel instead of two (the VM, 2026-10-06: a
#    quarter of the 3D's pixels differ from their 2x2 block at 2x, none at 1x), the 2D layers native, the layout the
#    same.
nds melonds - 40 move:0.70,0.50 tap:0.70,0.50 -- Legend of Zelda, The - Phantom Hourglass*.zip
#
# Items 10 and 3, the TV cutout and the GB studio bezel (the picture track). key:ctrl+shift+b is the radial's
# Next Bezel (79/<index into the system's bezel variants>). Judge the shots at 1:1 and with crops zoomed 4x
# (magick SHOT -crop ... -filter point -resize 400%); the frame-60 capture of each launch is the emulator's state
# root's semu-render-final.ppm.
#
# Item 10. In every shot of T1 to T5: (a) no light line round the TV's opening: the lip (the band between the
# curved picture and the opening) is as dark as the TV's own plastic beside it, under the same night light (on the
# PS1 in integer bezel the top lip about #181714 beside #1C1B19 plastic, where M15 drew a 3 px #626261 line on a
# #333332 lip), and the game's colours mirror onto it, sharp at the picture's edge and fading outward; (b) every
# curved picture edge is smooth at 4x: where the curve crosses a pixel row or column the edge pixel shades
# gradually, with no notch, no step that jumps back, and no dark or coloured dash along the edge (the NTSC fringe
# rows and columns of GDV-NTSC run unbroken), in the integer and the non-integer states alike. G1 is item 3.
#
# T1. Item 10, the PS1 (the owner's report; F2 left it on integer bezel). before: integer bezel. after-1 (81/2,
#     FIT: NON-INTEGER GAME) and after-2 (81/3, FIT: NON-INTEGER BEZEL): each held to (a) and (b); crop the
#     top-left corner of the opening and the left edge at mid-height.
psx retroarch mednafen_psx 45 key:ctrl+shift+f key:ctrl+shift+f -- Crash Bandicoot (USA).m3u
# T2. Item 10, the N64 on both TVs ("a lot of aliasing with the tv curve on a few consoles"). before: the Living
#     room CRT; after-1 (79/1, BEZEL: CRT WITH SPEAKERS): the speakers TV; both held to (a) and (b).
n64 retroarch mupen64plus_next 40 key:ctrl+shift+b -- Super Mario 64 (USA).zip
# T3. Item 10, the Genesis. before and after-1 (the next Fit state): (a) and (b).
genesis retroarch genesis_plus_gx 25 key:ctrl+shift+f -- Aero the Acro-Bat (USA).zip
# T4. Item 10, the GameCube in Dolphin (the standalone path through the GL tap, the same compositor): (a) and (b).
gc dolphin - 50 key:ctrl+shift+f -- Legend of Zelda, The - The Wind Waker (USA).rvz
# T5. Item 10, the Dreamcast in Flycast: (a) and (b).
dreamcast flycast - 40 key:ctrl+shift+f -- Crazy Taxi (USA).chd
# G1. Item 3, the GB studio bezel is gone. before: the DMG shell round the LCD. after-1 (79/1, BEZEL: OFF): no
#     shell, the picture alone on pure black; the toast never names STUDIO GRAY, and Next Bezel offers DMG and OFF
#     only (the variants file's bezels=dmg|none). choices gb {"bezel_variant":"none"}.
gb retroarch gambatte 25 key:ctrl+shift+b -- Tetris (World) (Rev 1).zip
#
# Items 4, 8 and 9 (the deck track). A case's result lists every framebuffer size the renderer composed into
# ("size: framebuffer WxH (was ...) at frame N", the first is the boot's), its frame rate every two seconds
# ("rate: N frames in T ms (F per second), longest gap, composing CPU ms mean and max, GPU ms mean and max", the
# last eight seconds) and, for a CRT shader, how many rows each scanline spans ("pitch: scanlines R rows each,
# intres D (L lines drawn H tall)"). boot-capture.sh films the same boots every 0.25 s (boot-capture.cases), and
# its --analyse marks any shot SHRUNK into the top left.
# Earlier cases leave their Fit and bezel choices in OUT/home/semu.json; these cases judge sizes, rates and
# scanlines that hold in every Fit state.
#
# D1. Item 4, the GameCube (Dolphin, the owner's example). size: one line only, "framebuffer 1280x800 (was 0x0) at
#     frame 1": Dolphin's render window is full screen before its core boots, so the first frame Semu composes is
#     already the screen's size and no later frame is smaller. before: Wind Waker's boot (the Nintendo logo or
#     its opening) inside the TV at full size, nothing drawn in a block in the top left with black to its right
#     and below. rate, read line by line in run.log against the shots (the bar is the game's own frame rate):
#     the Nintendo and Dolby logos, drawn at 60 frames a second, at least 59.8 per second with the longest gap under
#     50 ms (a first run compiles Dolphin's shaders and may drop a frame or two at a logo: 2026-10-06 logged 47.6 and
#     30.1 ms; a gap of 50 ms or more fails); the disc load into the opening is excused, at most two
#     lines under that bar with the longest gap under 200 ms (2026-10-06: 40.0 and 29.3 per second, 83 and 145 ms);
#     from the opening on the game draws 30 frames a second and Dolphin presents only those (D14), so every line at
#     least 29.8 per second with the longest gap under 50 ms (one and a half of its frames; an even 30.0 is full
#     speed).
gc dolphin - 30 -- Legend of Zelda, The - The Wind Waker (USA).rvz
# D2. Item 4, the Wii (Dolphin): as D1, New Super Mario Bros. Wii's strap or title.
wii dolphin - 30 -- New Super Mario Bros. Wii*.wbfs
# D3. Items 8 and 9, the Dreamcast (Flycast), the owner's Sonic Adventure with no input on its defaults (the TV,
#     GDV-NTSC (Dreamcast) since item 9) in the Fit state T5 left (non-integer game): the Sega logo, then the opening movie from about 20 s. The run
#     is at the Deck screen's own refresh (the result's gamescope line: 90 Hz on the OLED). size: one line, 1280x800
#     from frame 1. rate (run.log, a line every two seconds): the logo at least 59.8 per second with the longest gap
#     under 25 ms; the movie, which the game draws at 30 frames a second (Flycast shows only the frames the game
#     draws), at least 29.8 per second with the longest gap under 50 ms (one and a half of its frames). Before the
#     swap-interval patch the movie read 22.5 per second at 90 Hz, four refreshes a frame, with the game at 75%
#     (item 8); the composition's GPU time printed. pitch: "scanlines 2 rows each, intres 1.200 (480 lines drawn 800
#     tall)", 400 scanlines of 2 rows (on Sharp CRT, before item 9 moved the default, none: easymode declares no
#     intres, so the renderer sets nothing). before: the movie inside the TV.
dreamcast flycast - 90 -- Sonic Adventure (USA) (En,Ja,Fr,De,Es) (Rev A).chd
# D4. Item 4, the PS2 (PCSX2): size: no line smaller than the last; before: Def Jam's boot at full size.
# OSD check: no framebuffer shrink after PCSX2 receives SIGINT.
ps2 pcsx2 - 40 -- Def Jam - Fight for NY (USA).iso
# D5. Item 4, the 3DS (Azahar through the Vulkan layer): size: no line smaller than the last; before: both
#     screens in the shell, never a smaller frame in a corner.
n3ds azahar - 40 -- Legend of Zelda, The - Ocarina of Time 3D*.3ds
# D6. Item 9, the NES on its new default, Retro Crisis's GDV-NTSC (NES - Clean). receipt shader_preset=
#     gdv-ntsc.slangp; before: Super Mario Bros. 3's title, soft NTSC colour without the old composite dot crawl;
#     pitch: "scanlines 2 rows each, intres 0.000 (240 lines drawn 480 tall)" at integer bezel; rate: 60 per
#     second, the GPU time beside the SNES's and Genesis's.
nes retroarch mesen 20 -- Super Mario Bros. 3 (USA) (Rev 1).zip
#
# Item 9, GDV-NTSC on the four 480-line consoles: their default since the Deck measured it (PLAN M16 item 9; Sharp
# CRT before, GDV-NTSC then one press away as gdv). Each case starts on the default, GDV-NTSC (DREAMCAST), (PS2) or
# (PS2 VALUES), steps the Fit states on with each Fit press, so the run covers every state, and ends with
# key:ctrl+shift+v, Next Shader: 80/1, SHADER: SHARP CRT (EASYMODE), the previous default one press away. Read every
# rate line in run.log (one each two seconds) against schedule's token times, not only the result's last four, and
# the pitch lines for each state: one row per line at integer bezel (intres 0, 480 lines drawn 480 tall) and
# "scanlines 2 rows each, intres 1.200 (480 lines drawn 800 tall)" at non-integer game (448 lines: intres 1.120).
# The bar that keeps a console on GDV-NTSC (the same in PLAN M16 item 9): in every state each rate line as Sharp
# CRT's in the same scene, at least 59.8 per second with the longest gap under 25 ms where the game draws 60 frames
# a second, with the GPU never the limit: gpu_busy_percent well under 100 and the clock under its 1600 MHz top. The
# composition's GPU time is read, not barred: the Deck runs its GPU at the slowest clock that keeps up, so the time
# grows as the clock drops (GDV-NTSC 5.4-6.0 ms at integer bezel and 7.3-7.8 ms in non-integer game where Sharp CRT
# took 1.7-1.9 and 2.8-3.0, at 200-1040 MHz, on 2026-10-06; the review round's 4 ms cap was this time, and it was
# not what limits a frame). A shot judged by eye: even scanlines, no bands, no mask grain; the PS2 values' red
# convergence offset (his deconvergence, 2 px) shows as a red edge on dark text on the PS2, GameCube and Wii.
# D7. The Dreamcast (Flycast, Sonic Adventure). Earlier cases left it in non-integer game: before, GDV-NTSC there;
#     after-1 (81/3) non-integer bezel; after-2 (81/0) integer game; after-3 (81/1) integer bezel; after-4 (80/1)
#     Sharp CRT at integer bezel. choices dreamcast {"shader_variant":"sharp","placement":"bezel"}. These shots fall
#     in the opening movie, which the game draws at 30 frames a second, so the rate bar here is D3's for the movie
#     (at least 29.8 per second, the longest gap under 50 ms).
dreamcast flycast - 90 key:ctrl+shift+f key:ctrl+shift+f key:ctrl+shift+f key:ctrl+shift+v -- Sonic Adventure (USA) (En,Ja,Fr,De,Es) (Rev A).chd
# D8. The PS2 (PCSX2, Def Jam), from integer bezel (no earlier Fit press on the PS2): before, GDV-NTSC at integer
#     bezel; after-1 (81/2) non-integer game; after-2 (81/3); after-3 (81/0); after-4 (81/1) integer bezel; after-5
#     (80/1) Sharp CRT. S3 left the PS2 at render scale 2x (receipts render_scale=2), a stricter run than the
#     default 1x: a pass here holds at 1x; a miss only here is rerun at 1x (Reset twice, Restart Game, then D8).
ps2 pcsx2 - 60 key:ctrl+shift+f key:ctrl+shift+f key:ctrl+shift+f key:ctrl+shift+f key:ctrl+shift+v -- Def Jam - Fight for NY (USA).iso
# D9. The GameCube (Dolphin, The Wind Waker), in the state T4 left (non-integer game): before, GDV-NTSC there;
#     after-1 to after-3 step on to integer bezel as D7; after-4 (80/1) Sharp CRT.
gc dolphin - 60 key:ctrl+shift+f key:ctrl+shift+f key:ctrl+shift+f key:ctrl+shift+v -- Legend of Zelda, The - The Wind Waker (USA).rvz
# D10. The Wii (Dolphin, New Super Mario Bros. Wii), in the state F1 left (integer game): before, GDV-NTSC at
#     integer game; after-1 (81/1) integer bezel; after-2 (81/2) non-integer game; after-3 (81/3); after-4 (81/0);
#     after-5 (80/1) Sharp CRT at integer game.
wii dolphin - 60 key:ctrl+shift+f key:ctrl+shift+f key:ctrl+shift+f key:ctrl+shift+f key:ctrl+shift+v -- New Super Mario Bros. Wii*.wbfs
# D11. Item 8 in play, on the owner's own Dreamcast shader (CRT Royale, the owner's semu.json): Sonic Adventure at the
#     Deck screen's own refresh (90 Hz on the OLED). D7 left Sharp CRT and integer bezel, so key:ctrl+shift+v
#     journals 80/2 (SHADER: CRT ROYALE). start at 30 s skips the opening movie to the title (after-1, at least 59.8
#     per second with the longest gap under 25 ms), whose attract mode then plays Sonic's story scene against E-101
#     (from about 65 s) and Speed Highway's demo (about 131 to 175 s), both drawn by the game at 30 frames a second:
#     every line inside a scene at least 29.8 per second with the longest gap under 50 ms (a load between scenes
#     reads lower). The game at full speed, not 75%: in the Speed Highway shots (about after-17 to after-22) the
#     HUD's TIME gains 6.00 s from one shot to the next, 6 s apart (crop the picture's top left at 4x). key:shift is a
#     lone Shift, which neither Semu nor Flycast binds: a filler that keeps the case running with a shot every 6 s.
#     Before the swap-interval patch (90 Hz, 2026-10-06): the scenes at 22.5 per second and the HUD gaining 1.87 s in
#     2.5 s. choices dreamcast {"shader_variant":"royale","placement":"bezel"}.
dreamcast flycast - 30 start key:ctrl+shift+v key:shift key:shift key:shift key:shift key:shift key:shift key:shift key:shift key:shift key:shift key:shift key:shift key:shift key:shift key:shift key:shift key:shift key:shift key:shift key:shift key:shift key:shift key:shift -- Sonic Adventure (USA) (En,Ja,Fr,De,Es) (Rev A).chd
# D12 to D15. Item 9's comparison in one run each: GDV-NTSC, then Sharp CRT, on the same game a minute apart (the
#     measurement that moved the default ran each shader in a home of its own, the same scenes at the same times; PLAN
#     M16 item 9). Reset to Defaults (85/1, RESET TO DEFAULTS: 79/0 80/0 81/1) puts the console on GDV-NTSC at integer
#     bezel; four shots later Fit (81/2) moves it to non-integer game, the larger picture; four shots later Next Shader
#     (80/1, SHADER: SHARP CRT (EASYMODE)) the same state on Sharp CRT. Bar: every rate line of a 60-frame scene at
#     least 59.8 per second with the longest gap under 25 ms on both shaders, and the GPU never the limit
#     (gpu_busy_percent well under 100, the clock under 1600 MHz; a sampler beside the run, or system-matrix.sh's
#     lines); the composition's GPU time printed for each window (2026-10-06, at 200-1040 MHz: GDV-NTSC about 5.5 ms at
#     integer bezel and 7.5 ms in non-integer game, Sharp CRT about 1.8 and 2.9). A load between scenes reads lower on
#     either shader. Shots judged by eye: GDV-NTSC brighter and smoother, no mask grain, scanlines whole rows.
# D12. The Dreamcast (Sonic Adventure): start at 30 s reaches the title (60 per second), whose attract mode plays the
#     story scene at 30 frames a second from about 48 s (its bar: at least 29.8 per second, gaps under 50 ms).
dreamcast flycast - 30 start key:ctrl+shift+r,ctrl+shift+r key:shift key:shift key:shift key:shift key:ctrl+shift+f key:shift key:shift key:shift key:shift key:ctrl+shift+v key:shift key:shift key:shift key:shift -- Sonic Adventure (USA) (En,Ja,Fr,De,Es) (Rev A).chd
# D13. The PS2 (Def Jam): S3 left it at render scale 2x, so Restart Game (87/1) follows Reset and the game boots again
#     at 1x (receipts render_scale=1) on GDV-NTSC at integer bezel; the bar holds once it draws. Run in a fresh home
#     (already 1x) nothing waits, so the press journals 87/0 and the game keeps running.
# OSD check: reset and restart keep the framebuffer at 1280x800 through teardown.
ps2 pcsx2 - 40 key:ctrl+shift+r,ctrl+shift+r key:ctrl+shift+d key:shift key:shift key:shift key:shift key:shift key:shift key:ctrl+shift+f key:shift key:shift key:shift key:shift key:ctrl+shift+v key:shift key:shift key:shift key:shift -- Def Jam - Fight for NY (USA).iso
# D14. The GameCube (The Wind Waker): the title at 30 frames a second, its attract scenes after it.
gc dolphin - 40 key:ctrl+shift+r,ctrl+shift+r key:shift key:shift key:shift key:shift key:ctrl+shift+f key:shift key:shift key:shift key:shift key:ctrl+shift+v key:shift key:shift key:shift key:shift -- Legend of Zelda, The - The Wind Waker (USA).rvz
# D15. The Wii (Mario Kart Wii): its attract race from about 50 s, the heaviest scene measured (a run's first race
#     after a new release compiles Dolphin's shaders and stutters on either shader: 2026-10-06's first run on Sharp CRT
#     missed 41 of 55 lines, the warm runs a handful at track changes).
wii dolphin - 40 key:ctrl+shift+r,ctrl+shift+r key:shift key:shift key:shift key:shift key:ctrl+shift+f key:shift key:shift key:shift key:shift key:ctrl+shift+v key:shift key:shift key:shift key:shift -- Mario Kart Wii (USA)*.wbfs
# R1. RetroArch's pace at Game Mode's refresh (M16's Deck probe; Super Mario World's title and its attract demo, no
#     input). result: "sound: into the null sink" (input-check.sh's default since the probe: with the sound cut
#     RetroArch had no audio device to wait on and drew 180 frames in 2 s, 90 a second, the game at 1.5 times its speed;
#     every RetroArch case of the M16 final run read that). run.log: "semu-retroarch: screen 90.00 Hz, content 60.10
#     fps, 1.50 refreshes a frame: frames timed at the content's own rate (Sync to Exact Content Framerate)" (any
#     screen reading within a hertz of 90; no line at all: the video context reports no rate and RetroArch's own sync
#     stays, the release before the patch). rate: every line from the title on 60.0 to 60.2 per second with the
#     longest gap under 26 ms (measured with the setting in a trial tree: 121 frames in 2013 ms on every line, gaps
#     17.5-25.6 ms; RetroArch's own sync read 59.8 to 60.4 with gaps to 29 ms, its frames in bursts of PipeWire's
#     period). key:shift is a lone Shift, which neither Semu nor RetroArch binds (Select is the right Shift): a filler
#     that keeps the case running with a shot every 6 s. Shots: the title, then Mario's attract demo, no RetroArch text.
snes retroarch snes9x 30 key:shift key:shift key:shift key:shift key:shift key:shift key:shift key:shift -- Super Mario World (USA).zip
